# Value Steward mk II — daily cron template.
#
# NOT installed anywhere by checking this file in. Customize the paths below,
# then install with `crontab crontab` (or merge into an existing crontab).
# `crontab -l` is the only source of truth for what is actually scheduled;
# this file and the live schedule drift, and have before.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE COMMAND, MANY TICKS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# run_daily does whichever of its two jobs is outstanding, so every line below
# is the same command. What differs is only when it fires:
#
#   16:15–17:00  the market is shut, so the latest COMPLETED session is today.
#                Today's decisions are computed and logged. Nothing is sent.
#   09:45–15:45  the market is open, and the latest completed session is still
#                YESTERDAY. Yesterday's logged decisions are submitted, at the
#                first tick that succeeds; every later tick that day is a
#                no-op.
#
# Deciding needs a finished close, which does not exist until the bell rings —
# so an order can only ever be placed in the following session. Submitting
# during regular hours, rather than leaving a market order queued into the
# opening auction, is a deliberate choice: the opening print is the least
# predictable price of the day, and a queued order takes whatever it gives.
#
# The retry windows are wide on purpose. A daily bar is not published the
# instant the market closes, and a single missed tick must not cost a session.
# That width means cron WILL start a second invocation before a slow first one
# finishes on some days — that is the design, and it is exactly why every
# attempt after the first has to be a genuinely harmless no-op rather than
# usually one. Three mechanisms make that true together:
#
#   * a single-instance file lock (run_lock.py) — "is another attempt in
#     flight right now";
#   * the once-per-day guards (decision_log / execution_log) — "did this
#     already happen";
#   * deterministic client_order_ids — the broker itself rejects a duplicate
#     submission of an order that actually landed, so the last line of defence
#     is not our bookkeeping.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEFORE INSTALLING, CHECK TWO THINGS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. THE CLOCK. These times are US Eastern. Confirm the machine agrees
#    (`date`; `timedatectl` if present). If it reads UTC, the 16:15 line fires
#    at 12:15 ET — the middle of the session — and the decide window never
#    lands after a close. The code itself is safe either way (it reads the
#    broker's calendar in Eastern and refuses an unfinished session), but the
#    schedule would be pointless. Convert the times if the clock is not ET.
#
# 2. THE VM HAS TO SURVIVE. On WSL, installing this crontab is not enough:
#    WSL2 shuts the whole distro down when its last session closes, and cron
#    goes with it. `crontab -l` and `systemctl status cron` will both still
#    look correct afterwards, because they describe a machine that is no longer
#    running. Keep a WSL window open, or set vmIdleTimeout / a Task Scheduler
#    keepalive. Measured 2026-08-14: closing the window after Thursday cost
#    Friday's session in the first dry-run week — one of six.
#
# 3. NOTIFICATIONS. Two channels, and they cover different things.
#
#    Push (ntfy) is the primary one: two notifications a trading day, on the
#    same topic value-steward uses. It needs VS_NTFY_TOPIC in .env — with it
#    unset, pushes are silently off, which is fine for a dev box and wrong
#    for the run. Check .env before arming.
#
#    MAILTO is the backstop, and catches what a push cannot: run_daily exits
#    non-zero on a failed order or a missed session, and cron mails the
#    output — including a Python traceback, which is the one failure mode a
#    push written in that same process can never report on itself.
#
MAILTO=you@example.com

# `mkdir -p logs` is load-bearing, not tidiness: the shell sets up the `>>`
# redirect BEFORE python runs, so if logs/ does not exist the redirect fails,
# the command never executes, and the run produces no output at all — the
# quietest possible failure. logs/ is gitignored, so a fresh clone has none.

# --- DRY RUN (default). Computes and logs decisions; sends nothing. ---
45 9,10,11,13,14,15 * * 1-5 cd /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2 && mkdir -p logs && /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/.venv/bin/python -m vs2.run_daily >> /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/logs/run_daily.log 2>&1
15,30,45 16 * * 1-5 cd /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2 && mkdir -p logs && /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/.venv/bin/python -m vs2.run_daily >> /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/logs/run_daily.log 2>&1
0 17 * * 1-5 cd /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2 && mkdir -p logs && /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/.venv/bin/python -m vs2.run_daily >> /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/logs/run_daily.log 2>&1

# --- ARMED. Replace the three lines above with these to let it trade. ---
#
# Do this only after the dry-run week has shown sessions.jsonl gaining one
# DECIDED row per session, with a sane invested_fraction, and no STALE_BARS.
#
# 45 9,10,11,13,14,15 * * 1-5 cd /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2 && mkdir -p logs && /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/.venv/bin/python -m vs2.run_daily --execute >> /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/logs/run_daily.log 2>&1
# 15,30,45 16 * * 1-5 cd /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2 && mkdir -p logs && /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/.venv/bin/python -m vs2.run_daily --execute >> /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/logs/run_daily.log 2>&1
# 0 17 * * 1-5 cd /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2 && mkdir -p logs && /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/.venv/bin/python -m vs2.run_daily --execute >> /home/YOUR_USER/value-steward-2/logs/run_daily.log 2>&1

# --- The universe is NOT refreshed on a schedule, deliberately. ---
#
# DESIGN.md calls the Dow 30 membership "refreshed monthly", and index
# membership does change. But a universe that shifts mid-run changes the
# benchmark under the measurement, which is a worse problem than a stale
# constituent for sixty sessions. Refresh it by hand between runs:
#
#   .venv/bin/python -m vs2.data.index_membership
#
# and record that you did, so the review knows which universe it covers.
